{"name":"Harmonic Tan","hex":"#c1b287","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"harmonic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to harmony.","Pleasant to hear; harmonious; melodious."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A component frequency of the signal of a wave that is an integer multiple of the fundamental frequency.","The place where, on a bowed string instrument, a note in the harmonic series of a particular string can be played without the fundamental present."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/harmonic"},{"term":"tan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["The trigonometric function tangent."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Tangale."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tan"}],"entries":[{"title":"Harmonic","blocks":[{"text":"A harmonic is a sinusoidal wave whose frequency is a positive integer multiple of the fundamental frequency in a periodic signal. The fundamental counts as the first harmonic; the others are higher harmonics. Since all harmonics are periodic at the fundamental frequency, the sum of harmonics is also periodic at that frequency. The set of all possible harmonics is the harmonic series."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Moodswingerscale.svg/330px-Moodswingerscale.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic","wikidata":"Q1148098"},{"title":"Harmony (color)","blocks":[{"text":"In color theory, color harmony is a property of certain aesthetically pleasing color combinations. These combinations create pleasing contrasts and consonances that are said to be harmonious. These combinations can be of complementary colors, split-complementary colors, color triads, or analogous colors. Color harmony has been a topic of extensive study throughout history, but only since the Renaissance and the Scientific Revolution has it seen extensive codification. Artists and designers make use of these harmonies in order to achieve certain moods or aesthetics."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmony_(color)","wikidata":"Q5659234"},{"title":"Tan (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Tan is a pale tone of brown. The name is derived from tannum used in the tanning of leather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg/330px-Chestnut_Oak_Bark.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tan_(color)","wikidata":"Q1670336"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Cello_natural_harmonics.png/500px-Cello_natural_harmonics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Cello_natural_harmonics.png/1280px-Cello_natural_harmonics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Cello_natural_harmonics.png/500px-Cello_natural_harmonics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two different notations of natural harmonics on the cello. First as sounded (more common), then as fingered (easier to sightread).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACello_natural_harmonics.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Pipe001.gif/500px-Pipe001.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Pipe001.gif/500px-Pipe001.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APipe001.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Molecule1.gif/500px-Molecule1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":